{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795770736,"nonce":"HRlV37GANMRWBGb+s/pMBGOphEc=","title":"Inset box shadows do not look as in a browser","message":"GitHub #86. box-shadow: 0 0 20px #b8bcb5 inset on a 120x50 box draws with diagonal creases from the corners and about twice the browser's strength at the edges.\n\nThe inner shadow shader takes the distance to the nearest edge, so its falloff has a ridge along each diagonal. It starts at full strength at the edge and ends with a smoothstep over blur + spread. An offset only scales the brightness towards one side. The geometry is the border box, where CSS uses the padding box. The circle inner shadow does the same.\n\nCSS draws the colour times one minus the coverage of a hole, blurred by a Gaussian whose deviation is half the blur radius. The hole is the padding box moved by the offset and shrunk by the spread. That model fits the browser picture of the GitHub issue to within 3 of 255 at every sampled point.","files":null}]}